  "query_preview": "..(Friday through Wednesday)\nIssued at 503 PM EDT Wed Aug 5 2026\n\nThe long term forecast period gets off to a wet start, with \nmid/upper level troughing digging deeper into the Greater Ohio River \nValley from Friday into Saturday. Embedded shortwave disturbances \nare progged to propagate through this troughing pattern and towards \nthe forecast area in this time frame. The presence of these impulses \nwill bolster the areal coverage of these days' showers and storms \nbeyond the typical isolated to scattered summertime pop-ups \nexperienced earlier in the week. While the related increase in cloud \ncover should keep highs in the mid 80s, the highest precipitation \nchances will still align with the diurnal heating/instability cycle. \nPoPs generally fall in the numerous to widespread coverage \ncategories on Friday (55-75%) and Saturday (50-80%) thanks to the \nbetter support aloft. \n\nMost of this late week convective activity will come in the form of \ngeneric thunderstorms, as modeled wind shear remains below the 20 \nknot threshold. Severe thunderstorms are unlikely, but high \ntemperatures in the 80s and dewpoints in the 70s will yield 1500+ \nJ/kg of SB-CAPE in the afternoon and evening hours. Ensemble mean \nsoundings continue to resolve high freezing levels above 15k ft and \ncloud layer winds in the 15-20kt range with saturated thermodynamic \nprofiles. PWATs remain in the 1.6 to 1.8 inch range in this time \nframe, and all of the above suggests that these storms will be \nefficient rainmakers. Given the potential for antecedent rainfall \nduring the short term forecast period, there could be pockets of \nalready-saturated soils scattered around the CWA before the better \nforcing arrives this weekend. This means that the rain from Friday \nand Saturday's storms could prove excessive. Isolated instances of \nflash flooding will be possible in locales that experience multiple \nrounds of slow-moving storms multiple days in a row. WPC has \naccordingly maintained Marginal (Level 1/4) EROs for the entire JKL \nCWA on both Friday and Saturday  \n\nRecent forecast guidance continues to suggest that the disturbance \nresponsible for Saturday's activity will be better-defined than the \nprevious impulse on Friday. On the backside of that second, \nrelatively sharper shortwave trough, the flow aloft will adopt a \nmore zonal orientation. That quasi-zonal flow persists into the \nearly states of next week and advects a drier continental airmass \ninto the midlevels. At the same time, a ridge will be amplifying \nacross the Southwestern and Central CONUS. The resultant midlevel \nheight rises and subsidence indicate that Eastern Kentucky will get \na break from the more widespread rain chances on Sunday, but this \ncomes with a cost. With less clouds in the sky, efficient diurnal \nwarming should allow highs to climb into the upper half of the 80s \non Sunday, Monday, and Tuesday. While PWATs (mean values between 1.4 \nand 1.6 inches) will be relatively lower than they were this \nweekend, there will still be enough humidity for widespread apparent \ntemperatures in the 90s. A few heat index readings near 100 degrees \ncannot be ruled out, and the atmosphere may still be able to squeeze \nout a few summertime pop-up showers/storms during the afternoon and \nevening hours on Sunday and Monday. Rain chances ramp back up again \non Tuesday and Wednesday, but the specifics of that precipitation \nforecast remain shrouded in uncertainty. Model spread begins to \nincrease in the latter portions of the forecast period. It is \nplausible that shortwave disturbances will propagate around the \nnortheastern periphery of the ridge and bring chances for more \norganized storms to the forecast area. The highest late-period PoPs \nare generally resolved over northern portions of the forecast area, \nand this aligns with some of the available extended-range convective \nguidance. Trends in that guidance will be monitored in future \nforecast packages, and interested parties are accordingly encouraged \nto stay tuned for updates.",
